#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221188 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "99f2f8a2-f5aa-5a32-b29f-8e1f79f504cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21692" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/cd/cd022d759a5ce5a8521687d42eaf38053339212301a6b3559ad4bee935f69317.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f5/f51930ce883e8e2f4865d846b38c475e68875565593ad58044125e81f0443718.svg)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/53/53cec1a04bca84fba279c48cf5a531674040c07e2a1dac12748107b91809ff72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/05/05e9d31a8413096e60f8d30b8206052375dc7c9df5895568a33b475fca22fc72.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221189 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e61881ec-4361-554d-b374-35b8b3f64366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21693" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/33/33beedce1869f8f3a894434acc7c0a89a94062470e7dacd8a5e14a2d61ef36b5.svg) and ![](images/ac/ac4b467f8441d1473393728f7e9a05fd36b106044e73c151780b963db77bdbfa.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/7b/7b764933668ae61643bf324c8a1275df1f5c2ca778153103e687f2ce77719c5d.svg)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221190 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/id #uuid "9215f93a-14ad-5be5-9f27-25ba556252e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21694" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\nI. $y=3x+3$\n\nII. $y=-x$\n\nIII. $y=3x$\n\nIV. $y=-3x$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "414dfe6a-1f20-5fe7-ad95-3fc99d444dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305794} {:answer/id #uuid "4e927cc3-051a-5ae1-b0d7-84857bde2483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=3{u}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305918} {:answer/id #uuid "73064f9f-4959-5d53-9867-b81b772186aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)-3v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306244} {:answer/id #uuid "a70fda98-7770-50fe-904c-9274ab9f7d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306724} {:answer/id #uuid "c51297de-90e1-536b-a15c-4f9cd32e0340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=u(t)-3{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "414dfe6a-1f20-5fe7-ad95-3fc99d444dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305794} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc339c5-d3fa-5203-ac77-6f9b945ab99c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a3c0b53-eab9-5b58-92da-6bae03210acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333671" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}(t)-2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+2)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1-2u(t)-2+v(t)+2-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+4(v(t)+2)-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-{v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)-4+4v(t)+8-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00580f00-c9c9-518e-a439-751daeb07fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305155} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5ad691-7a73-5889-842f-111db46dc768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305424} {:answer/id #uuid "602f3036-f235-5026-9df9-a73fa52bbdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306078} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0e9b0d-7cbe-5eb2-a096-a56813b36aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306885} {:answer/id #uuid "f21f6a45-d91d-5614-9474-10c0869ecfac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00580f00-c9c9-518e-a439-751daeb07fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305155} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5fbb819-bf00-5059-8545-ab6b4de01c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc805b5f-d7a6-53b7-9121-090baedc8eb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333683" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=2x-3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f2c097-7bd7-557a-a116-a684e091c780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553049} {:answer/id #uuid "591aca34-5191-5fe1-9db7-320758bc5e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553061} {:answer/id #uuid "a85a19cd-59c2-5d28-9c89-e1a6319ddd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553206} {:answer/id #uuid "c4582499-f706-5cc9-8e35-eb95cf03cc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553258} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d4bacd-b2da-531e-8234-b3604fea9dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f2c097-7bd7-557a-a116-a684e091c780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553049} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd3e00c-418a-520a-80fa-a7b949ac974e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a810631-1bae-5445-bc88-4aea289acecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335078" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08447618-2120-543d-83ec-e215ccd38852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305228} {:answer/id #uuid "6184b1d4-ffa4-5e81-823d-f62432c74d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306092} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce2d05-ee85-52fd-8d2c-7910d844e7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306874} {:answer/id #uuid "c54f359c-411e-55e2-a259-418b96a3cdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306989} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5e6628-28e3-5ecf-b5ce-4530143b5b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c54f359c-411e-55e2-a259-418b96a3cdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306989} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb0a724-01dd-5634-9aae-6102654ba4f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff48fa6b-fda8-5ef9-88ce-a7737fd92d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-335079" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfe2ae9-aec8-50cf-849c-115d91f5165f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552950} {:answer/id #uuid "786ae3e8-66e4-5326-b092-7941c56f8c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553113} {:answer/id #uuid "82a270b6-94b8-5f5f-8060-a6d5207f6169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553130} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6f2836-2dba-56ff-9d23-d8bc722d1e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553336} {:answer/id #uuid "fae32f0d-4f34-5b6e-8153-95de23dd818e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb6f2836-2dba-56ff-9d23-d8bc722d1e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553336} :answer-field/id #uuid "9249f96b-64b8-59e4-9bf4-1d116673b015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b02e6de2-bb8c-5f1a-89b9-7e259d335937" :question/math-academy-id "q-335195" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e663ec7-657b-5405-ab38-7c06c516cceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t)+5 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309221} {:answer/id #uuid "4678bd53-05f1-5959-8fb9-a11a90f9898c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309286} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8310c1-24f9-5674-839e-ccb79fd7f7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t)-1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310397} {:answer/id #uuid "e9159d47-2f62-50dd-bc0c-dd1d39d72b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310600} {:answer/id #uuid "f8633208-5163-55dd-ad12-2957a0ceeb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8633208-5163-55dd-ad12-2957a0ceeb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310728} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdca0da8-a855-5f66-8cb7-f7ff71aac855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ec6cfc1-e844-58cb-9d89-165931802f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-335207" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a6070f-c849-53e5-a30d-7451121042ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309303} {:answer/id #uuid "49ae912e-69ab-533a-9aca-d467e61f39e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309311} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfc0bb2-d8e0-582a-930e-1a7d166d8678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309439} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d019a-27d2-516c-9bba-990266da31a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309893} {:answer/id #uuid "f15addc4-0325-5db2-9004-cd4515b1d0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)+5 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)-9\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49ae912e-69ab-533a-9aca-d467e61f39e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309311} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ad45ec-0c42-5e94-921d-81dcd713c823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f5893f7-3112-574c-a435-c387df346ccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335213" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+5y(t)-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a827329-a0ff-550f-bb7e-ee23bd0d15dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552947} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcfc10f-6abc-5282-81a9-6b42e5fe2cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553153} {:answer/id #uuid "ac44864b-4825-549b-a5af-a7b6b9f978f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553214} {:answer/id #uuid "c691a6d3-d876-51c8-b913-9e1a8fbf2d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553263} {:answer/id #uuid "d0155f97-773b-5d90-9876-d81f9a834c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac44864b-4825-549b-a5af-a7b6b9f978f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553214} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc81f40-30f6-5eea-8198-03adefd62b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65e63d99-f774-5779-ae22-f06fdcf67250" :question/math-academy-id "q-335373" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9289b9d6-6c26-59a9-9339-03db3b0e6487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309917} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdfb3ed-cf42-5da0-9780-5187fbbb613f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310015} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8b0ece-8ad2-5087-a105-3570cab5339e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310153} {:answer/id #uuid "f9910be5-a41d-55a6-8460-83633449918f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2{u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310738} {:answer/id #uuid "fc85b89a-ed36-56cd-a344-b20f510141c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cdfb3ed-cf42-5da0-9780-5187fbbb613f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310015} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e4dad82-fde3-5dbe-b8dc-0a002b198cc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4393b51d-33cc-55b0-9d58-a2715d679766" :question/math-academy-id "q-335377" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+3(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+1)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+3u(t)-3+v(t)+1+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-3(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+2(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+2v(t)+2-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f0b6ab-4885-5330-a0b0-4a9829224535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308996} {:answer/id #uuid "4a203876-c908-58c9-a1b4-915014694b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=u(t)+2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309315} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb11d7a-e1d7-5326-b79e-3a5c5e8f9f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2{u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309715} {:answer/id #uuid "9ede7e00-15d8-5ef7-a919-32f356c47aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2{u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-2{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310031} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e46ffc-1f78-5163-82a3-4779461c5f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20f0b6ab-4885-5330-a0b0-4a9829224535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308996} :answer-field/id #uuid "38bf6211-ed01-5b45-a702-dc4ff88a4235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97fce750-06d1-52ae-bf98-2a8f014cf2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335384" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+4(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+2)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+4u(t)-4+v(t)+2+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+8(v(t)+2)-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+8v(t)+16-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5760a8e4-95a8-56ad-9ea9-5dd8de11365f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)-3\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431869} {:answer/id #uuid "6e795028-7b52-57a5-b776-1234735687ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431871} {:answer/id #uuid "898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431874} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08dcf3-62ce-5a52-845e-1dd4af782784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431877} {:answer/id #uuid "f0064606-8a67-5c4c-866a-5588c435a49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3{u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431874} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f2b70f-bcb2-5554-a4f5-52982774bf66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1db4f53-42d8-55c9-8ba8-b7dde90ef735" :question/math-academy-id "q-335386" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,3]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system’s equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+3,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+3)^{2}+6(v(t)+3)-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+6v(t)+9)+6v(t)+18-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+3)-6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+2u(t)+2+v(t)+3-6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b1c10e-a98d-5f25-8201-5bd029b54514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308945} {:answer/id #uuid "708413f5-a6fa-5181-88a8-58e02fc5b6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309622} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9a977a-b3e9-5ea4-a84c-950dd99123e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309723} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f190a5-7b93-5c5f-be5c-382899ebd516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310050} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc6afa7-65aa-586b-a53d-e79b9f5f3712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}-‖{x}^{∗}‖" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c9a977a-b3e9-5ea4-a84c-950dd99123e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309723} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bbfc305-5209-5774-be14-ec3f40c5a885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ddd0c95-9d32-5926-af86-97c6ac159ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335485" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[-2,\\,4]^{T}.$ The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvalue of the matrix $A?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvalues as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ or any expressions that are equal to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only quantity that is guaranteed to be an eigenvalue of $A$ is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff26a1b-ad1f-5d05-89cc-02f56d59942b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308861} {:answer/id #uuid "27a4595e-09af-54d9-a231-89de87202b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309048} {:answer/id #uuid "8d375117-19a1-56a7-a442-2827ca48d8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309861} {:answer/id #uuid "b336f18c-05ea-5473-b9f9-d275a8dc8e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310198} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f559d-11e3-513c-8b87-7349ad6ffd4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b336f18c-05ea-5473-b9f9-d275a8dc8e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310198} :answer-field/id #uuid "b559932a-b36e-51c6-a295-7216c795b0ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8143e4f-528e-5eef-b81d-03c2d102e474" :question/math-academy-id "q-335488" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=-4x+1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfies the above conditions is $y=-4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f81b54-2c42-5068-ae9b-f58486c9e0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly one equilibrium point." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552903} {:answer/id #uuid "473c449b-404f-5472-957c-dab54c4302e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly two equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553016} {:answer/id #uuid "a855d4d0-c845-5d6d-a652-d3eb8a9d050f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has infinitely many equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553205} {:answer/id #uuid "be71f679-d451-5899-a53b-df4726edebc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has no equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553246} {:answer/id #uuid "f395de86-b03b-5a4b-97b8-39a4f1f73ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly three equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f395de86-b03b-5a4b-97b8-39a4f1f73ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly three equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553346} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebef8073-cadc-5d1c-afa4-6048d68ae08d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76bd165c-3511-57a4-8be2-2180b605d203" :question/math-academy-id "q-335520" :question/problem "A nonlinear system of ODEs has exactly three equilibrium points.  The system is then shifted using the transformation $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ where ${x}^{∗}$ is one of the equilibria. Which of the following statements about the number of equilibria in the shifted system is guaranteed to be true?" :question/worked-solution "A shift of coordinates is a translation of the phase space and does not alter the structure of the system’s equilibrium set. Each equilibrium point of the original system is translated to a corresponding equilibrium in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, although one of the equilibria is moved to the origin, the total number of equilibrium points remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"The shifted system still has exactly three equilibrium points.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f3bf54-299d-519d-8e6c-2af83e18ba67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has infinitely many equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308878} {:answer/id #uuid "325b7548-1fd3-5808-9688-d105cd1b935b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly one equilibrium point." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309131} {:answer/id #uuid "343100fe-3a69-5b2f-856d-5e0e7c8d7bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has no equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309144} {:answer/id #uuid "5640025b-8ddc-56fe-9650-e4af6154ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309397} {:answer/id #uuid "f3815e27-d942-56b3-bd0d-dc050b7b92f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly three equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5640025b-8ddc-56fe-9650-e4af6154ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309397} :answer-field/id #uuid "363ca1ab-6fa9-5126-9a3d-9fbbf088e283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9156c54-a0f8-59e7-b01b-764ac89d4134" :question/math-academy-id "q-335522" :question/problem "A nonlinear system of ODEs has exactly two equilibrium points. The system is then shifted using the transformation $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ where ${x}^{∗}$ is one of the equilibria. Which of the following statements about the number of equilibria in the shifted system is guaranteed to be true?" :question/worked-solution "A shift of coordinates is a translation of the phase space and does not alter the structure of the system’s equilibrium set. Each equilibrium point of the original system is translated to a corresponding equilibrium in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, although one of the equilibria is moved to the origin, the total number of equilibrium points remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points.\""}]}
